-/

-- Proof sketch: for `K.IsPerfect → IsCompactObject K`, combine Lemma `15.79.1`, which identifies
-- perfect objects with the triangulated subcategory generated by `R[0]`, with the Chapter 13
-- owner API showing that compact objects form a saturated triangulated subcategory and that
-- `R[0]` is compact. For the converse, apply compactness to the truncation/direct-sum argument
-- mediated by Lemma `15.79.2` to obtain a bounded-above finite free approximation to `K`; then
-- factor `𝟙 K` through the resulting perfect object and use the retract-closure of perfect objects
-- from Lemma `15.75.5`.
/-- Proposition 15.79.3: for an object `K` of `D(R)`, being perfect is equivalent to being a
compact object. -/
theorem isPerfect_iff_isCompactObject (K : DMod) :
    K.IsPerfect ↔ IsCompactObject K := sorry

end

end CategoryTheory
